course: dictionary
lesson: 12564
title: A word for claiming first
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Dibs» is slang, which means it is an informal word people use in everyday talk, not in school essays. When you say «Dibs!», you are claiming something before anyone else can. Context tells you this. «Dibs on the front seat!» shows that the speaker wants the seat first.
explain: Read the words around it. «I call dibs on the last biscuit!» Call and last biscuit are the clues: everyone might want the biscuit, so dibs must mean you claim it first. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then swap your meaning in. "I claim the last biscuit first" still makes sense, so your meaning works.
explain: Dibs can also be a short shout on its own, an interjection. «Dibs!» means the same as «I got it first!» Children often use it in playground arguments, and you can hear the playful fight in the word. Other places have their own words for it: bagsy in the UK, and shotgun for the front car seat.
fact: Dibs is a slang word, so it is informal and fun to say.
fact: «Dibs» means a claim to have or do something first.
fact: «Dibs on» is a common phrase, as in «Dibs on the top bunk!»
fact: «Dibs» is also an old UK slang word for money.
fact: Bagsy (UK) and shotgun (for the front car seat) work like dibs.
exercise: «Dibs on the front seat!» What does dibs mean here? | You are giving the seat away | You are getting out of the car | *You are claiming the seat first
exercise: Ella shouts «Dibs!» before anyone else reaches the slide. What has she done? | She has broken the slide | *She has claimed the slide first | She has asked for a biscuit | She has gone down the slide backwards
exercise: Find and copy the word that means claiming something first in «Dibs on the top bunk!» | *dibs | top | bunk | the
test: Jack shouts «I call dibs on the blue cup!» What is Jack doing? | Washing the cup | *Making a claim to the cup first | Giving the cup away
test: Mia shouts «Shotgun!» to get the front seat. Which word works in the same way as shotgun? | Tired | Sorry | Hungry | *Dibs
exam: «Dibs!» is a short shout. What does it show? | A joke about the weather | *A claim to something first | A sign that you are tired
